.SH NAME

pdfannotation - view, add, change or remove text annotations

.
.
.
.SH SYNOPSIS

.TP 14
.BI pdfannotation " " [ "-o output.pdf" ] " " [ "-a text" ] " " [ "-r" ] \
" " [ "-l" ] " " [ "-d" ] " " [ "-v" ] " " [ "-h" ]
.BI "" file.pdf " " page " ["x " " y | "search" ]

.
.
.
.SH OPTIONS AND ARGUMENTS

.TP
.BI -o " output.pdf
output file; defaults to
.I file-annot.pdf

.TP
.BI -a " text
the text of the new annotation

.TP
.B -r
remove or change an annotation instead of adding a new one

.TP
.B -l
print location and icon of annotations

.TP
.B -d
print the annotated text

.TP
.B -v
verbose

.TP
.B -h
help

.TP
.I file.pdf
input file

.TP
.I page
page number

.TP
.BI "" x " " y | search
position


.
.
.
.SH DESCRIPTION

With no arguments, all text annotations in the page are printed. With option
\fI-a\fP, a text annotation is added at the top-left corner. With option
\fI-r\fP alone, the annotation is removed. With both, the annotation is
changed.

A position can be given in two alternative ways: either as cooordinates
.I x y
or as a text to search for.
This position is used when adding, removing,
changing and viewing annotations:

.TP
adding
the new annotation is placed this exact point

.TP
changing or removing
the annotation that is changed or removed is the closest to this point

.TP
viewing
only the annotation closest to this point is shown

.
.
.
.SH SEE ALSO

.I pdfannot(1)

